To center a `div` both horizontally and vertically, you can use CSS. Here are a few methods:

### 1. Centering Horizontally
To center a `div` horizontally, you can use the following CSS:

```css
div {
    margin: 0 auto; /* Horizontal centering */
    text-align: center; /* If you want to center text inside the div */
}
```

### 2. Centering Vertically
For vertical centering, you need to set the `display` property of the parent container to `flex` or `grid`. Here are two examples:

#### Using Flexbox:
```css
.parent {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center; /* Aligns items horizontally in the center */
    align-items: center; /* Aligns items vertically in the center */
    height: 100vh; /* Example height, adjust as needed */
}

.child {
    /* Your other styles */
}
```

#### Using Grid:
```css
.parent {
    display: grid;
    place-items: center; /* Centers both horizontally and vertically */
    height: 100vh; /* Example height, adjust as needed */
}

.child {
    /* Your other styles */
}
```

### Example HTML:
```html
<div class="parent">
    <div class="child">
        Centered Div
    </div>
</div>
```

### Explanation:
- **Flexbox**: The `justify-content: center;` property centers items horizontally, and `align-items: center;` centers them vertically within the flex container.
- **Grid**: The `place-items: center;` property centers items both horizontally and vertically in the grid container.

Adjust the `height` property of the parent container as needed to ensure it has a defined height for vertical centering to work.

